---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Gas Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-gas-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Heat Pump Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-heat-pump-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Tankless Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-tankless-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Types of Water Heaters",
    "url": "/types-of-water-heaters/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Water Heater Brands",
    "url": "/water-heater-brands/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How Long Do Water Heaters Last?",
    "url": "/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-19", modified: "2026-10-19" });
const title = "7 Best Electric Water Heaters: Tank and Hybrid Picks by Household Size";
const description =
  "The best electric water heaters for every household, compared on first-hour rating, UEF, element wattage and warranty, from 40-gallon tanks to 80-gallon hybrids.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "rheem-performance-platinum-50-electric",
    "name": "Rheem Performance Platinum 50-Gallon Electric",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "50 Gal, Dual 5,500 W Elements, 12-Year Warranty",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Performance Platinum is the electric tank we would put in most family homes, because it pairs a strong first-hour number with the longest standard warranty in the mainstream Rheem range. Rheem's spec sheet lists the 50-gallon medium model with PlusOne controls (XE50M12EC55U1) at a 63-gallon first-hour rating and a 0.93 UEF, with 25 gallons per hour of recovery at a 90-degree rise. That first-hour figure is what decides whether the last shower of the morning runs warm, and 63 gallons comfortably covers a household of three to four. Rheem's product summary for the electric Performance line cites up to a 12-year tank and parts warranty and up to three years of in-home labor, and a retailer listing for the 50-gallon Gladiator version states a 12-year limited warranty. The labor term varies by exact model, so read the certificate for the unit you buy. The catch is price: the 12-year models cost noticeably more than six-year tanks with identical tanks of water, so you are paying mainly for the warranty and the controls. For an owner who plans to stay put, that is money well spent.",
    "pros": [
      "63-gallon first-hour rating and 0.93 UEF on the 50-gallon medium model, per Rheem's spec sheet",
      "25 GPH recovery at a 90°F rise, per the same sheet",
      "Up to 12-year tank and parts warranty and up to 3 years in-home labor across the line, per Rheem",
      "Widely stocked, so a replacement or part is easy to find"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more than six-year tanks that hold the same water",
      "Labor coverage depends on the exact model number",
      "Higher-wattage elements on some versions may need a larger circuit, so check the nameplate"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For most homes, the Rheem Performance Platinum 50-gallon is the electric water heater to buy: a 63-gallon first-hour rating, a 0.93 UEF and up to a 12-year warranty, per Rheem."
  },
  {
    "id": "ao-smith-signature-100-50-electric",
    "name": "A.O. Smith Signature 100 50-Gallon Electric (E6-50R45D)",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "category": "50 Gal Short, Dual 4,500 W Elements, 6-Year Warranty",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Signature 100 is the value pick, and the one most Lowe's shoppers will see first. Lowe's lists the 50-gallon short model E6-50R45D with two 4,500-watt elements, a 0.92 UEF and a 62-gallon first-hour delivery, which is within a gallon of our top pick. Its warranty is a six-year limited tank and parts guarantee with one year of limited labor, per the same listing. The short height suits basements with low ceilings, closets under stairs and replacements where the old tank was squat. You give up the long warranty and the smart controls of premium tanks, and that is the whole trade: the water you get in the first hour is nearly the same. Other Signature 100 variants exist, including a 5,500-watt version listed with a nine-year warranty, so match the model number on the box to the one you meant to buy, and confirm the first-hour figure on A.O. Smith's own spec sheet, since the Lowe's listing was the only source we found for it.",
    "pros": [
      "62-gallon first-hour delivery and 0.92 UEF, per the Lowe's listing",
      "Dual 4,500 W elements suit the common 30 A circuit",
      "Short height fits low basements and closets",
      "Six-year tank and parts warranty plus one year of labor, per Lowe's"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only six years on the tank",
      "First-hour figure comes from a retailer listing, not A.O. Smith's own sheet",
      "Several Signature 100 variants with different warranties are sold side by side"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The A.O. Smith Signature 100 E6-50R45D gives you nearly the hot water of a premium tank, a listed 62 gallons in the first hour, for less money, in exchange for a six-year warranty."
  },
  {
    "id": "rheem-marathon-50-electric",
    "name": "Rheem Marathon 50-Gallon Electric (MR50245)",
    "badge": "Longest Warranty",
    "category": "50 Gal Tall, Polybutene Tank, Lifetime Tank Warranty",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Marathon is the electric heater for people who never want to replace a tank again. Instead of a glass-lined steel tank that an anode has to protect, it uses a non-metallic tank that cannot rust, and Ferguson lists a lifetime limited tank warranty with a six-year limited parts warranty. Rheem's Marathon spec sheet lists the tall 50-gallon MR50245C at a 0.91 UEF and a 57-gallon first-hour rating with 21 gallons per hour of recovery, and a rated capacity of 48 gallons. Other listings for the MR50245 give 61 gallons and a 0.94 energy factor, and one distributor shows a five-year tank warranty, so the figures depend on the exact suffix; confirm with the certificate for your model. The Marathon costs more than an ordinary tank and its first-hour number is slightly lower than our top pick, but you will replace elements and thermostats over the decades rather than the whole heater. For a house you plan to keep for good, that changes the long-term math.",
    "pros": [
      "Non-metallic tank that cannot rust, with a lifetime limited tank warranty and six-year parts warranty, per Ferguson",
      "57-gallon first-hour rating and 21 GPH recovery on the MR50245C, per Rheem's spec sheet",
      "No anode to replace",
      "Well insulated, per Rheem's description of the line"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more than a standard tank",
      "Slightly lower first-hour rating than our top pick",
      "Specs and warranty differ between model suffixes, so verify yours"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Rheem Marathon is the electric water heater to buy if you never want to replace the tank: a rust-proof tank with a lifetime limited tank warranty, per Ferguson."
  },
  {
    "id": "bradford-white-re350s6-electric",
    "name": "Bradford White RE350S6 50-Gallon Short Electric",
    "badge": "Best Pro-Installed",
    "category": "50 Gal Short, Dual 4,500 W Elements, Hydrojet",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Bradford White sells only through plumbers and wholesalers, so this is the pick if a contractor is supplying and fitting the heater. Retailer listings describe the RE350S6 as a 50-gallon short tank with dual 4,500-watt elements at 240 volts (3,500 watts at 208 volts) and a UEF of 0.92, with one listing showing 0.93. First-hour figures vary between listings at 60 and 63 gallons, and several give about 21 gallons per hour of recovery at a 90-degree rise. The Hydrojet sediment-reducing system is the feature Bradford White leans on: a listing describes it as reducing sediment, increasing first-hour delivery and minimizing temperature build-up in the tank. It carries a six-year limited tank warranty per the listings, and at roughly 47 inches tall it fits the same low spaces as our value pick. You cannot easily buy one at a big-box store, but if your plumber stocks Bradford White, it is a dependable choice.",
    "pros": [
      "Dual 4,500 W elements and a 0.92 UEF, per retailer listings",
      "Hydrojet system aimed at reducing sediment build-up",
      "About 47 in tall, suits low spaces",
      "Sold and supported through professional installers"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not sold at big-box stores",
      "First-hour figure varies by listing (60 or 63 gallons)",
      "Six-year tank warranty only"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If a plumber is supplying the heater, the Bradford White RE350S6 is a solid 50-gallon short tank, listed with dual 4,500 W elements, a 0.92 UEF and Hydrojet sediment control."
  },
  {
    "id": "rheem-performance-40-electric",
    "name": "Rheem Performance 40-Gallon Electric (XE40M06ST45U1)",
    "badge": "Best for Small Households",
    "category": "40 Gal Medium, Dual 4,500 W Elements, 6-Year Warranty",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Plenty of households do not need 50 gallons, and the 40-gallon Rheem Performance is the sensible size for one to three people or a smaller home. Rheem's product page lists the XE40M06ST45U1 at a 0.93 UEF with a six-year limited tank and parts warranty and a height of 53.75 inches. HD Supply lists a 53-gallon first-hour delivery and dual 4,500-watt elements, and describes it as suiting households of two to four. A smaller tank costs less to buy and loses a little less heat standing, though the real saving is modest because standby loss on a well-insulated tank is small. The medium height makes it a straightforward swap for most older 40-gallon heaters. If anyone in your house takes long showers back to back, step up to a 50-gallon model, because a 40-gallon tank is the one that runs out first.",
    "pros": [
      "0.93 UEF and six-year tank and parts warranty, per Rheem",
      "53-gallon first-hour delivery, per HD Supply",
      "Dual 4,500 W elements on a common circuit",
      "Medium 53.75 in height swaps easily for most 40-gallon tanks"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Runs short for larger families or back-to-back long showers",
      "Only six years on the tank",
      "First-hour figure comes from a retailer listing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For one to three people, the Rheem Performance 40-gallon is the right-sized, low-cost electric tank, listed at a 0.93 UEF with a six-year warranty."
  },
  {
    "id": "rheem-proterra-50-hybrid",
    "name": "Rheem ProTerra 50-Gallon Hybrid Heat Pump (PROPH50 T2 RH375-30)",
    "badge": "Most Efficient",
    "category": "50 Gal Heat Pump Hybrid, 3.88 UEF",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "If you are replacing an electric tank anyway, a heat pump hybrid is the upgrade that pays back, and the ProTerra is the one we would look at first. Rheem's page for the PROPH50 T2 RH375-30 lists a 3.88 UEF and says the line runs from 3.75 to 4.07 UEF depending on configuration, roughly four times the efficiency of a standard element tank. A retailer lists a 67-gallon first-hour delivery and 27 gallons per hour of recovery for this model, which is more hot water in the first hour than any standard tank here. The cost is the purchase price, the space and the location: a heat pump needs room around it, pulls heat from the air so it cools the space it sits in, and needs a condensate drain. Our full guide to heat pump water heaters covers the placement rules; read it before you buy, because a hybrid in a cramped closet will not deliver its rated efficiency.",
    "pros": [
      "3.88 UEF on this model and up to 4.07 across the line, per Rheem",
      "67-gallon first-hour delivery and 27 GPH recovery, per a retailer listing",
      "Still runs as a standard electric tank when demand is high"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs much more up front than a standard tank",
      "Needs space, airflow and a condensate drain",
      "Cools the room it sits in, which matters in a heated living space"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If your space allows it, the Rheem ProTerra hybrid is the most efficient electric water heater here, with a 3.88 UEF and a 67-gallon first-hour delivery listed for this model."
  },
  {
    "id": "ao-smith-proline-80-hybrid",
    "name": "A.O. Smith ProLine 80-Gallon Hybrid (FPTU-80)",
    "badge": "Best for Large Households",
    "category": "80 Gal Heat Pump Hybrid, 84-Gallon First Hour",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Large households with soaking tubs or several bathrooms in use at once need more than 50 gallons, and the standard 80-gallon electric tanks we checked have largely been replaced by hybrids. A.O. Smith's own standard 80-gallon electric ProLine (EGR-80) is listed as discontinued, with the hybrid named as its replacement. Retailer listings for the 80-gallon ProLine hybrid FPTU-80 series give an 84-gallon first-hour rating and a six-year limited tank warranty, and note two heating elements that help it recover quickly during heavy demand. That is the most hot water in the first hour of anything on this list. It carries the same caveats as any heat pump model, and at 80 gallons it is a large, heavy tank that needs a solid floor and plenty of room. If you only need an extra few gallons, a 50-gallon hybrid with a higher first-hour rating may be all you need.",
    "pros": [
      "84-gallon first-hour rating, per retailer listings",
      "Heat pump efficiency with two backup elements for fast recovery",
      "The natural replacement for discontinued standard 80-gallon electric tanks"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Large, heavy and expensive",
      "Six-year tank warranty only, per the listings",
      "Needs the same space, airflow and drain as any hybrid"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a big household, the A.O. Smith ProLine 80-gallon hybrid delivers the most hot water here, with an 84-gallon first-hour rating listed by retailers."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Electric Water Heaters",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-electric-water-heater/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best electric water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes, the Rheem Performance Platinum 50-gallon, which Rheem's spec sheet lists at a 63-gallon first-hour rating and 0.93 UEF, with up to a 12-year warranty across the line. For lower cost, the A.O. Smith Signature 100 50-gallon is listed by Lowe's at a 62-gallon first-hour delivery with a six-year warranty."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does an electric water heater last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Electric tanks typically last around 10 to 15 years, longer on average than gas tanks because there is no burner heating the tank bottom. Anode condition, water hardness and sediment shorten or extend that. A non-metallic tank such as the Rheem Marathon is designed not to rust, and carries a lifetime limited tank warranty per Ferguson."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size breaker does an electric water heater need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A common 4,500-watt, 240-volt heater draws about 18.75 amps, and installers typically put it on a 30-amp two-pole breaker with 10-gauge copper wire, because water heaters are treated as continuous loads. Higher-wattage elements can need more. Always follow the nameplate and installation manual, and have an electrician confirm."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size electric water heater do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "As a guide, one to two people suit a 30 to 40-gallon tank, three to four people a 50-gallon tank, and five or more a 65 to 80-gallon tank or a high-output hybrid. Compare first-hour ratings against your busiest hour, because electric tanks recover slowly once they run low."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a heat pump water heater worth it over a standard electric tank?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often, if you have the space. On our stated assumptions, a 3.88 UEF hybrid uses roughly a quarter of the electricity of a 0.93 UEF tank, which can save several hundred dollars a year. It costs more up front and needs airflow and a condensate drain, so check placement and local rebates first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does it cost to run an electric water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on use and your rate. Heating 50 gallons a day by 70 degrees at a 0.93 UEF works out to roughly 3,350 kWh a year, or about $570 at 17 cents per kWh. Lower thermostat settings, pipe insulation and fixing hot-water leaks all reduce it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I install an electric water heater myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A like-for-like replacement is within reach of a handy homeowner, but many areas require a permit, a properly piped relief valve and sometimes a drain pan or strapping. Fill the tank completely before restoring power. Hire a plumber or electrician for size changes, hybrids or any circuit work."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "best electric water heater",
      "electric water heater",
      "electric hot water heater",
      "best electric hot water heater",
      "50 gallon electric water heater"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Electric <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Water Heaters</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Seven electric tanks and hybrids, compared on the numbers that decide whether the last shower runs warm: first-hour rating, recovery, efficiency and warranty.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#rheem-performance-platinum-50-electric"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#first-hour" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. First-Hour Rating</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing by Household</a>
          <a href="#elements-circuit" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Elements and Circuit</a>
          <a href="#efficiency" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. UEF and Running Cost</a>
          <a href="#heat-pump" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Heat Pump Hybrids</a>
          <a href="#lifespan" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Warranty and Lifespan</a>
          <a href="#install" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Replacement and Install</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best electric water heater for most homes is a 50-gallon tank with a first-hour rating in the low 60s and a warranty long enough to match how long you plan to stay. Tank size matters less than the first-hour rating, standard electric tanks differ more in warranty than in efficiency, and if you have the space, a heat pump hybrid cuts the running cost to a fraction.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not install these heaters in a lab. We compared the numbers manufacturers and retailers publish for each model: the first-hour rating, the uniform energy factor (UEF), element wattage, warranty terms, and the dimensions that decide whether a tank fits the space your old one left. Where two sources gave different figures for the same model, we say so in the product card rather than picking whichever number looked better.
          </p>

          <p>
            We focused on electric tank heaters, the kind most homes without gas already have, and the kind most people searching for an electric water heater are replacing. We included two heat pump hybrids because they are the obvious upgrade when an electric tank fails, and because for larger households they have largely replaced standard 80-gallon electric tanks. Electric tankless heaters are a different decision with different electrical demands, and they have <a href="/best-electric-tankless-water-heater/">their own guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brand mattered less than you might expect. As our guide to <a href="/water-heater-brands/">water heater brands</a> explains, a few manufacturers sit behind most of the names on the shelf, and the tank inside a six-year model is often very close to the one in a twelve-year model. What changes is the warranty, the elements, the controls and the anode. We weighted warranty heavily for that reason: it is the clearest signal of how long the maker expects the tank to last.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also favored models you can actually buy. Several electric tanks that look good on paper are discontinued or sold only in specific regions, and an 80-gallon standard electric model we would once have recommended is now listed as discontinued with a hybrid as its replacement. Every pick here was listed for sale when we checked, but stock and model suffixes change often, so confirm the exact model number before you order.
          </p>
