        <div id="first-hour" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. First-Hour Rating Beats Tank Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            The number printed in big type on a water heater box is the tank size, and it is the least useful number on it. Two 50-gallon heaters can deliver noticeably different amounts of usable hot water, because what matters is how much hot water the heater produces in an hour of heavy use starting from a full, hot tank. That is the first-hour rating, and it appears on the yellow EnergyGuide label and on most spec sheets.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first-hour rating combines what is stored with what the elements can reheat during that hour. Electric heaters recover slowly compared with gas, because a pair of 4,500-watt elements, which normally run one at a time, puts far less heat into the water than a gas burner does. That is why an electric 50-gallon tank typically lands in the high 50s to low 60s on first-hour gallons, while the same size of gas tank often rates higher. Our picks range from 53 gallons for the 40-gallon Rheem to 63 gallons for the Rheem Performance Platinum, with the hybrids higher still.
          </p>

          <p>
            To use the number, estimate your peak hour. A shower typically uses around 10 to 20 gallons depending on the showerhead and its length, a dishwasher a few gallons, and a clothes washer on a warm cycle more again. Add up what happens in your busiest morning hour and buy a heater whose first-hour rating covers it with a little to spare. If two people shower back to back while the dishwasher runs, you are already past 40 gallons.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match the first-hour rating to your busiest hour, not the tank size to your household.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A heater that covers your peak hour will rarely run cold. Tank size alone does not tell you that, especially with slow-recovering electric elements.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

