        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing by Household
          </h2>

          <p>
            As a starting point, one to two people usually do fine with a 30 to 40-gallon electric tank, three to four people with a 50-gallon tank, and five or more with a 65 to 80-gallon tank or a hybrid with a high first-hour rating. Those are rules of thumb that manufacturers and retailers publish in some form, and they assume ordinary habits. A household of two with a large soaking tub can need more hot water than a family of four who take short showers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric heaters push you toward the upper end of those ranges because of slow recovery. When a gas tank runs low it can be back to usable temperature fairly quickly; an electric tank with a single 4,500-watt element running reheats at roughly 20 to 25 gallons per hour at a 90-degree rise, which is what our picks' spec sheets list. If your house runs out of hot water now, going up a tank size is usually more effective than any other change.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the space before you choose a size. A 50-gallon tank comes in short, medium and tall versions, and the short models such as the A.O. Smith Signature 100 and the Bradford White RE350S6 are about 47 inches tall, while tall versions run well over 60 inches. Measure height, diameter, and the clearance the installation instructions require around the tank and above it for the connections. Larger 80-gallon tanks are heavy when full, so make sure the floor can carry them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about how long you will be in the house. If you expect to move within a few years, a well-priced six-year tank sized correctly is the sensible buy. If you plan to stay, the extra cost of a longer warranty, a rust-proof Marathon tank or a heat pump hybrid is spread across many more years of use.
          </p>

