        <div id="elements-circuit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Elements, Wattage and the Circuit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most residential electric tanks have two elements, an upper and a lower, and in typical wiring only one runs at a time. The upper element heats the top of the tank first so you get some hot water quickly, then hands over to the lower element, which does most of the work. Bradford White's listing for the RE350S6, for example, describes non-simultaneous operation, which is the normal arrangement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Element wattage decides recovery speed and the circuit you need. The common size is 4,500 watts at 240 volts. Simple arithmetic gives 18.75 amps for one element at 240 volts, and because a storage water heater is treated as a continuous load under the electrical code, installers generally size the circuit at 125 percent of that, which commonly means a 30-amp two-pole breaker and 10-gauge copper wire. Some models use 5,500-watt elements for faster recovery, which can push the required circuit higher.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is to read the heater's nameplate and installation manual, which state the required breaker and minimum wire size, and to match a replacement to your existing circuit where you can. Swapping a 4,500-watt heater for another 4,500-watt heater is the simple case. Moving to higher-wattage elements, or to an electric tankless unit, is where an electrician needs to confirm the panel and wiring can take it. Our <a href="/tankless-water-heater-cost/">tankless cost guide</a> covers how quickly those electrical upgrades add up.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are replacing a heater on a 208-volt supply, common in some apartments and commercial buildings, element output drops: the RE350S6 listing gives 3,500 watts per element at 208 volts against 4,500 at 240. Recovery is slower as a result, so a 208-volt home may want a larger tank to get the same usable hot water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="efficiency" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. UEF and Running Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            The uniform energy factor is the efficiency number you will see on every spec sheet. For standard electric tanks it clusters tightly, around 0.91 to 0.93 among our picks, because an electric element turns almost all of its energy into heat in the water; the losses are the heat that escapes through the tank walls and pipes. A higher UEF on a standard tank mostly means better insulation.
          </p>

          <p>
            To give a sense of scale, here is a rough estimate with stated assumptions rather than a measured figure. Heating 50 gallons a day by 70 degrees takes about 29,000 BTU, or roughly 8.6 kilowatt-hours of heat. At a 0.93 UEF that is about 9.2 kWh of electricity a day, or around 3,350 kWh a year. At 17 cents per kWh, that comes to roughly $570 a year. Your bill will differ with your water use, inlet temperature, thermostat setting and electricity rate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same arithmetic shows why heat pump hybrids matter. At a 3.88 UEF, like the ProTerra listed here, the same hot water would need roughly a quarter of the electricity, around 800 kWh a year, or about $140 at the same rate. On those assumptions the hybrid saves on the order of $400 a year, which is why it can pay back its higher price within the life of the tank. Real savings depend heavily on where the heater sits and how cold that space is.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Estimated Annual Electricity (50 gal/day, 70°F rise, 17¢/kWh)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~$570</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Standard tank, 0.93 UEF</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~$140</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Hybrid, 3.88 UEF</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~$430</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Estimated yearly gap</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8.6 kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Daily heat in the water</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Small habits move the bill too. Setting the thermostat to around 120 degrees rather than higher, insulating the first few feet of hot pipe and fixing dripping hot taps all reduce what the heater has to do. None of those changes depend on which model you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat-pump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When a Heat Pump Model Pays
          </h2>

          <p>
            A heat pump hybrid is an electric water heater that moves heat out of the surrounding air into the tank, using standard elements only as a backup. That is how the ProTerra reaches a 3.88 UEF and the A.O. Smith hybrid an 84-gallon first-hour rating listed by retailers. If you have the space, it is the most important upgrade to consider when an electric tank fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            The space is the catch. A hybrid needs a certain volume of air around it, a condensate drain, and enough room for the taller unit and the fan on top. It cools and dehumidifies the room it is in, which is a bonus in a warm garage or basement and a cost in a heated living space in winter. It also makes some fan noise. Our <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/">heat pump water heater guide</a> covers placement, noise and the 120-volt plug-in models that avoid electrical work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Upfront cost is higher, often much higher, than a standard tank. Utility rebates and programs can narrow that gap in some areas, so check what your utility offers before deciding. If the numbers work, the yearly savings can recover the difference within several years on the assumptions in our running-cost section. If they do not, or your only space is a cramped closet, a well-insulated standard tank such as the Rheem Performance Platinum is still a reasonable choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hybrids also have more parts that can fail, including the compressor, fan and controls, and their warranties sometimes differ between the tank and the heat pump components. Read the terms. A hybrid that saves $400 a year but needs a costly repair in year eight still usually comes out ahead, but you should know what is covered.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Warranty, Anode and Lifespan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric tanks tend to outlast gas ones. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/">how long water heaters last</a> puts electric tanks at roughly 10 to 15 years, against 8 to 12 for gas, largely because there is no burner heating the bottom of the tank. What usually ends a tank's life is corrosion, and that is where the anode rod comes in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Glass-lined steel tanks rely on a sacrificial anode that corrodes instead of the tank. When the anode is used up, the tank starts to corrode. Longer-warranty tanks generally come with heavier anodes or a second anode, which is a big part of what you pay for in a 12-year model over a 6-year one. Checking the anode every few years and replacing it when it is mostly gone is the cheapest way to extend any steel tank's life, especially with hard or softened water.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Rheem Marathon takes a different approach: its non-metallic tank cannot rust, so it has no anode and carries a lifetime limited tank warranty per Ferguson. You will still replace elements and thermostats over the years, as you would on any electric heater, but those are inexpensive parts compared with a new tank and an installation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, read the warranty terms before you need them. Many require registration within a set time, professional installation, or proof that the heater was maintained. Labor is often covered for only a year, if at all, so a warranty that replaces a failed tank may still leave you paying for removal and reinstallation. Draining a few gallons from the bottom once or twice a year reduces sediment, which matters more in hard-water areas.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Replacing an Electric Heater: Cost and Install
          </h2>

          <p>
            Replacing an electric tank with another electric tank of the same size and wattage is one of the simpler plumbing jobs: shut off power at the breaker and water at the supply, drain the old tank, disconnect the water lines and wiring, set the new tank, reconnect, fill completely before turning power on, and check for leaks. Turning the power on before the tank is full burns out the upper element almost immediately, which is the classic first-time mistake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many areas require a permit for water heater replacement, and codes usually require a temperature and pressure relief valve piped to a safe drain, a drain pan in some locations, and seismic strapping in earthquake zones. A plumber will handle those and haul away the old tank. If you are moving to a different size, a hybrid or a higher-wattage model, the job can involve electrical changes, a condensate drain or new piping, which is where professional installation is worth paying for.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a broader look at the options, our overview of the <a href="/types-of-water-heaters/">types of water heaters</a> compares tank, tankless, heat pump and solar side by side. If you have gas available, our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">gas water heater guide</a> covers tanks that recover faster than electric ones, at the cost of venting and combustion air.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever route you choose, keep the paperwork: the model and serial number, the installation date and the warranty registration. A warranty claim in year nine goes far more smoothly when you can show the heater was installed correctly and registered on time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by tank size alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two 50-gallon tanks can differ by several gallons of usable hot water in the first hour. Compare first-hour ratings, which reflect both storage and recovery, against your busiest hour of use, not the number on the front of the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Turning on power before the tank is full</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An element that heats with no water around it burns out within seconds. Fill the tank completely, open a hot tap until water runs steadily with no air, and only then restore power at the breaker.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heater with higher-wattage elements can need a larger breaker and heavier wire than the one it replaces. Read the nameplate, match it to your existing circuit, and have an electrician confirm any change before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never checking the anode</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The anode is what protects a steel tank from rusting through. Inspect it every few years and replace it when it is mostly eaten away, especially with hard or softened water, or the tank can fail long before its warranty would suggest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a hybrid in a tight closet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heat pump model needs a large volume of air, a condensate drain and room for the fan. Squeezed into a small closet, it runs on its backup elements and loses most of the efficiency you paid extra for.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

