          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best electric water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the Rheem Performance Platinum 50-gallon, which Rheem's spec sheet lists at a 63-gallon first-hour rating and 0.93 UEF, with up to a 12-year warranty across the line. For lower cost, the A.O. Smith Signature 100 50-gallon is listed by Lowe's at a 62-gallon first-hour delivery with a six-year warranty.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an electric water heater last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Electric tanks typically last around 10 to 15 years, longer on average than gas tanks because there is no burner heating the tank bottom. Anode condition, water hardness and sediment shorten or extend that. A non-metallic tank such as the Rheem Marathon is designed not to rust, and carries a lifetime limited tank warranty per Ferguson.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size breaker does an electric water heater need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A common 4,500-watt, 240-volt heater draws about 18.75 amps, and installers typically put it on a 30-amp two-pole breaker with 10-gauge copper wire, because water heaters are treated as continuous loads. Higher-wattage elements can need more. Always follow the nameplate and installation manual, and have an electrician confirm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size electric water heater do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As a guide, one to two people suit a 30 to 40-gallon tank, three to four people a 50-gallon tank, and five or more a 65 to 80-gallon tank or a high-output hybrid. Compare first-hour ratings against your busiest hour, because electric tanks recover slowly once they run low.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a heat pump water heater worth it over a standard electric tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, if you have the space. On our stated assumptions, a 3.88 UEF hybrid uses roughly a quarter of the electricity of a 0.93 UEF tank, which can save several hundred dollars a year. It costs more up front and needs airflow and a condensate drain, so check placement and local rebates first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run an electric water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on use and your rate. Heating 50 gallons a day by 70 degrees at a 0.93 UEF works out to roughly 3,350 kWh a year, or about $570 at 17 cents per kWh. Lower thermostat settings, pipe insulation and fixing hot-water leaks all reduce it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install an electric water heater myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A like-for-like replacement is within reach of a handy homeowner, but many areas require a permit, a properly piped relief valve and sometimes a drain pan or strapping. Fill the tank completely before restoring power. Hire a plumber or electrician for size changes, hybrids or any circuit work.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
